AI Skills & Portfolio Chatbot — RAG-Powered Assistant
A self-hosted RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) chatbot that answers questions about my skills, projects, and services in real time — pulled from a live knowledge base, not scripted or guessed.

Built as a working example of the same AI agent + knowledge base pattern used in client-facing support and enquiry automations.

The Problem
Most business websites are static. A visitor wants to know "have you worked in my industry?" or "what does this cost?" and the only option is a contact form and a wait. There's no way to get a real, accurate answer immediately — and most FAQ chatbots just recite a fixed script instead of understanding the question.

The Solution
This project gives an AI agent an actual, structured knowledge base to search before it answers:

A skills/portfolio document is uploaded, chunked, embedded, and stored in a vector database (Qdrant)
A separate chat-triggered AI agent receives visitor questions and searches that knowledge base for relevant context
The agent answers using only what it retrieves — grounded in real data, with instructions to say so honestly if the knowledge base doesn't cover something
Conversation memory keeps multi-turn chats coherent
The whole stack is self-hosted, authenticated, and runs with zero public database exposure

How It Works
The workflow has two independent pipelines sharing one Qdrant collection:

Ingestion (upload → store)
Form Upload (Files)
    │
    ▼
Qdrant Vector Store (insert mode)
    │
    ├── Default Data Loader (binary → text)
    │       │
    │       ▼
    │   Recursive Character Text Splitter (500 char chunks, 150 overlap)
    │
    └── Embeddings (OpenAI)

Retrieval (chat → answer)
Chat Message Received
    │
    ▼
AI Agent (GPT-4.1-mini + conversation memory)
    │
    ▼
Qdrant Vector Store (retrieve-as-tool mode)
    │
    └── Embeddings (OpenAI)
    │
    ▼
Grounded response back to the user

Insert and retrieval are handled by two separate Qdrant Vector Store nodes in the same workflow — one dedicated to writing new documents in, the other exposed to the AI Agent as a callable retrieval tool. This is the standard n8n pattern for RAG: the two operations have different node configurations and shouldn't be combined into one.

Tech Stack
n8n (self-hosted) — workflow orchestration, AI agent, and chat interface
Qdrant (self-hosted, Docker) — vector database for semantic search
Docker Compose — service orchestration alongside the existing n8n/Caddy stack
Caddy — reverse proxy, automatic HTTPS via Let's Encrypt
Cloudflare — DNS management
OpenAI Embeddings — text-to-vector conversion for both ingestion and query
OpenAI GPT-4.1-mini — response generation via the n8n AI Agent node
Recursive Character Text Splitter — chunking (500 characters, 150 overlap)

Key Notes
Qdrant runs with no ports published to the internet — only reachable internally by n8n over the Docker network, with API key authentication as a second layer.
The Qdrant dashboard is separately exposed behind its own subdomain, HTTPS, and Basic Auth for admin access only — kept fully apart from the application's internal traffic.
Letting n8n auto-create the Qdrant collection (rather than pre-creating it manually) avoids a named-vector mismatch — n8n's Qdrant node writes to an unnamed default vector, so manually created named-vector collections aren't compatible.
The AI agent's system prompt explicitly instructs it to search the knowledge base before answering and to admit when information isn't available — reducing hallucinated answers on factual details like pricing or project history.
The knowledge base is a living document — updated and re-embedded as new projects and services are added.

Roadmap
Hybrid search (dense + sparse vectors) for improved accuracy on exact-match queries like pricing and project names
Expand the knowledge base with individual project case studies, not just a single summary document
Embed the chat widget directly into the agency website for live visitor use

Files
workflow-ai-skills-chatbot.json — the full n8n workflow (ingestion pipeline + RAG chat agent, import-ready; credential IDs and webhook IDs are placeholders — reconnect your own on import)
Abiodun_Ogunleye_AI_Automation_Skills.pdf — the source knowledge base document

Setup
Self-host Qdrant via Docker Compose alongside your n8n instance, with no ports published externally
Import workflow-ai-skills-chatbot.json into n8n
Reconnect your own Qdrant and OpenAI credentials on both Qdrant Vector Store nodes
Let n8n auto-create the Qdrant collection on first insert run — don't pre-create it manually
Upload your knowledge base document via the form trigger to populate the collection
Activate the workflow and test via the chat trigger

Part of a series of practical AI automations built for UK small businesses.
